From 24e3de95fc494bc37ec1387c97678d2b25c42bbd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 05:51:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/20] feat(reconciler): redeploy a serving service when its
 spec changes

A PUT that changes the spec of a Ready service bumped its generation,
but nothing acted on it: the reconciler treated Ready, Degraded and
Updating as phases with nothing to do, so the service kept serving the
old spec with generation ahead of observedGeneration forever.

Ready and Degraded services whose generation is ahead of their
observedGeneration now move to Updating. The next pass stops the
previous deployment, takes an allocation for the new generation (the
held one is reused when it still satisfies the accelerator request,
otherwise it is released and a new one is taken) and starts the
deployment run of the new generation. Updating then follows that run
like Verifying does and ends in Ready, or in Failed when the run
fails. A spec change while Updating restarts the update with the
latest spec.

The update stops the old instance before it starts the new one, so
the service is unavailable from the Ready -> Updating write (the
gateway only routes to Ready services) until the new instance is
verified. Running both side by side would need a second allocation,
which a single-device inventory cannot provide.

The policy is evaluated once, when the update starts. A pass that the
policy blocks, or that lacks the scheduler or workflow runtime, writes
no status and only logs: the manager stamps observedGeneration with
the current generation on every status write, after which the update
would never be picked up again.

The state machine gains Degraded -> Updating so that a degraded
service is updated the same way.
